---
/**
 * Illusion 02 · The Reversal.
 *
 * The seven slots, filled and ported into the shared Shell exactly as the hero
 * is. Every batting average rendered below is real (Baseball-Reference); the
 * only thing the reader changes is the weighting, computed in their browser by
 * `scripts/the-reversal.ts`. No dataset, nothing server-rendered.
 *
 * Copy is an in-register draft, voiced against the figure. No em dashes in any
 * rendered string (the anthology sweep); the protected caveat is rendered with
 * a colon in place of its dash.
 */
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
import Slot from '../components/Slot.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Sidenote from '../components/Sidenote.astro';
import { bySlug, kickerOf } from '../lib/illusions';

const illusion = bySlug('the-reversal')!;
---

<Shell
  title={`${illusion.name} · Statistical Illusions`}
  description={illusion.lede!}
  illusion={illusion}
>
  {/* ── 1 · FRAME ── */}
  <Slot slot="frame" label={kickerOf(illusion)} heading={illusion.name} lede={illusion.lede}>
    <Sidenote title="Two hitters, three seasons.">
      <p>
        Two baseball players, David Justice and Derek Jeter, and the three seasons where their
        careers overlapped. One of them hit for the higher batting average in 1995. He did it again
        in 1996, and again in 1997. Then you add the seasons together and he loses.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing below is faked and no season is left out. The averages are the real ones, and you
        are shown all of them before you touch anything. <b>The only number you will change is how
        the at-bats are split across the seasons. You will still flip the winner.</b>
      </p>
      <Fragment slot="note">
        This is Simpson’s paradox. It carries a name because it keeps surprising people who do
        statistics for a living, which is the first sign it is worth ninety seconds of your
        attention.
      </Fragment>
    </Sidenote>
  </Slot>

  {/* ── 2 · SETUP — the real record, in full ── */}
  <Slot slot="setup" id="r-setup" label="Setup" title="The record">
    <div class="body">
      <p>
        Here is everything, both men, every season. Batting average is just hits divided by at-bats.
        The at-bats column is the one to keep an eye on: it is on the table, in plain sight, and it
        is also the number that is about to do all the work.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table aria-label="Justice and Jeter, batting by season">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Player</th><th>Season</th><th>At-bats</th><th>Hits</th><th>Average</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="r-rows"></tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="tbl-more">
        <span id="r-tblnote">The higher average each season is coloured.</span>
        <button class="btn ghost small" id="r-toggle">Add the 1997 season →</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="dare">
      <div class="l">What is fixed, and what you move</div>
      <p>
        Every batting average in that table is real and stays exactly where it is. Nothing is
        hidden from you now and nothing is revealed later that was withheld.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one thing you will change is how each man’s at-bats are distributed across his seasons.
        You are not editing the record; you are choosing how to weigh it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Slot>

  {/* ── 3 · OPERATE — re-weight the at-bats ── */}
  <Slot slot="operate" id="r-operate" label="Operate" title="Re-weight the seasons">
    <Sidenote title="Same averages, different weights.">
      <p>
        Drag the slider. At one end every season counts equally; at the other, seasons count in
        proportion to how many times the man actually batted, which is the real record and is marked
        <b>home</b>. Watch the two diamonds, each man’s combined average, slide along the axis.
      </p>
      <Fragment slot="note">
        No batting average changes as you drag, because none can. You are moving weight, not hits.
        The winner still crosses over.
      </Fragment>
    </Sidenote>

    <div class="rslider-wrap">
      <div class="rslider-ends">
        <span>Count each season equally</span>
        <span>As they actually played · <b>home</b></span>
      </div>
      <div class="rslider">
        <input
          type="range"
          id="r-slider"
          min="0"
          max="1"
          step="0.001"
          value="1"
          aria-label="Playing-time weighting, from every season equal to the real at-bats"
        />
        <span class="rflip" id="r-flip"><span class="rflip-lab">the total flips here</span></span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="justify" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="jl">What this weighting assumes</div>
      <p class="jt" id="r-just-reason">Move the slider. Both ways of combining the seasons are legitimate; that is the whole problem.</p>
      <div class="jc" id="r-just-cost"></div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      id="rfield"
      viewBox="0 0 880 300"
      title="Two hitters on one axis"
      question="each diamond is a combined average"
      ariaLabel="A horizontal batting-average axis with two lanes, Justice above and Jeter below. Each season is a dot sized by at-bats; a diamond marks each man's combined average and moves as the weighting changes."
      caption="Fig. 1 · the real season batting averages, on one axis. Each dot is a season, sized by the at-bats behind it; the diamond is that man’s combined average at the current weighting. Slide it and the diamonds move; nowhere does a batting average change."
    >
      <Fragment slot="readout">
        <span class="mono" style="color:var(--faint)">move the slider to compare the totals</span>
      </Fragment>

      <Fragment slot="legend">
        <div class="lg"><span class="rsw rsw-jus"></span> Justice</div>
        <div class="lg"><span class="rsw rsw-jet"></span> Jeter</div>
        <div class="lg"><span class="rdiamond"></span> combined average (weighted)</div>
        <div class="lg"><span class="mono lgk">dot size =</span> at-bats that season</div>
      </Fragment>
    </Figure>
  </Slot>

  {/* ── 4 · COMMIT — name the better hitter, one way ── */}
  <Slot
    slot="commit"
    id="r-commit"
    label="Commit"
    title="Name the better hitter"
    lockNote="· unlocks once you move the slider"
    locked
  >
    <div class="body">
      <p>
        Stop wherever you find defensible and say who was the better hitter over these seasons. Pick
        the one you would put your name to. There is no unpick button, here or in a published table.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="control rnames">
      <button class="btn ghost rname" id="r-name-justice">Justice was better</button>
      <button class="btn ghost rname" id="r-name-jeter">Jeter was better</button>
      <span class="ctlnote">One way. Choose the weighting you would defend, then choose the man.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="pubcard" id="r-pubcard" aria-live="polite">
      <span class="stamp">On the record</span>
      <p class="finding" id="r-finding">–</p>
      <div class="meta" id="r-pubmeta"></div>
    </div>
  </Slot>

  {/* ── 5 · REVEAL — the same figure, slid home ── */}
  <Slot
    slot="reveal"
    id="r-reveal"
    label="Reveal"
    title="The at-bats you weren’t weighing"
    lockNote="· unlocks when you commit"
    locked
  >
    <div class="revealline">
      <p class="r">You changed no batting average. You changed the weights, and the winner changed with them.</p>
    </div>

    <Sidenote title="Simpson (1951) · Blyth (1972)">
      <p id="r-revealbody">
        The figure has not changed its axis or its data. The at-bats behind each season are simply
        drawn to scale now, and the weighting has slid to the real record.
      </p>
      <Fragment slot="note">
        Simpson set the paradox out in 1951; Blyth gave it its name in 1972. It is arithmetic, not a
        trick: a ratio of sums can point the opposite way from every one of its parts.
      </Fragment>
    </Sidenote>

    <div class="counters" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="ctr">
        <div class="cl">Seasons Justice hit for the higher average</div>
        <div class="cv" id="r-c-seasons">–</div>
        <div class="cn">every season shown</div>
      </div>
      <div class="ctr">
        <div class="cl">Winner once the at-bats are pooled</div>
        <div class="cv" id="r-c-total">–</div>
        <div class="cn">the real, weighted average</div>
      </div>
      <div class="ctr">
        <div class="cl">Batting averages you changed</div>
        <div class="cv" id="r-c-changed">–</div>
        <div class="cn">the number that makes this honest</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Slot>

  {/* ── 6 · REPAIR — which number is right ── */}
  <Slot
    slot="repair"
    id="r-repair"
    label="Repair"
    title="So which number is right"
    lockNote="· unlocks after the reveal"
    locked
  >
    <Sidenote title="Pearl (2014).">
      <p>
        The lazy version of this lesson is “so always break the data down.” That is false, and it is
        the first thing a statistician will test you on. Whether to split or to pool is not a
        statistical question at all. It is a question about cause.
      </p>
      <Fragment slot="note">
        Pearl showed which number is correct depends on where the third variable sits in the causal
        story, and that sometimes the answer is in neither table.
      </Fragment>
    </Sidenote>

    <div class="control">
      <button class="btn" id="r-split">So which is it here? →</button>
      <span class="ctlnote">There is a real answer for baseball, and it is not “always split.”</span>
    </div>

    <div class="body repaired" id="r-repairbody" hidden>
      <p>
        The rule is about cause. If the third variable decided who got which treatment, the split
        numbers are right. If the treatment caused the third variable, pooling is right. And if
        something nobody measured is still in play, neither table is right.
      </p>
      <p>
        At-bats are none of those. No hidden bias handed one man his playing time, and batting more
        did not change how well either man hit. So neither number here is wrong. The per-season
        answer tells you who was the better hitter in a season; the pooled answer tells you who got
        more hits per at-bat across the span. They answer two different questions. The table only
        ever showed you one of them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Slot>

  {/* ── 7 · DEBRIEF — absence, authority, caveat ── */}
  <Slot
    slot="debrief"
    id="r-debrief"
    label="Debrief"
    title="What just happened"
    lockNote="· unlocks after the repair"
    locked
  >
    <div class="body">
      <p>
        You flipped a verdict about two men’s careers without altering one digit of their record.
        Everyone who has ever quoted the combined number and everyone who has ever quoted the
        per-season one was reading the same twelve numbers and answering a different question, mostly
        without noticing there was a choice to make.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="debrief">
      <div class="dblock">
        <div class="dl">What was absent from the table</div>
        <p class="dt">
          Every batting average was in front of you. What was never made visible was how unevenly the
          two men’s at-bats were spread across their good and bad seasons. That single omission is
          enough to reverse the winner, with nothing hidden and nothing faked. It is the variable
          nobody split by.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="dblock">
        <div class="dl">Who caught this</div>
        <p class="dt">
          <b>Edward Simpson</b> set the paradox out in 1951 (<i>JRSS-B</i> 13(2), 238). <b>Colin
          Blyth</b> gave it its name in 1972 (<i>JASA</i> 67(338), 364). <b>Judea Pearl</b> settled
          which number is correct, and when, in 2014 (<i>The American Statistician</i> 68(1), 8).
          The clinical version the argument leans on is Julious &amp; Mullee (<i>BMJ</i> 309, 1480,
          1994). The baseball line is from Baseball-Reference; the example is a favourite of Ken
          Ross’s <i>A Mathematician at the Ballpark</i>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="dblock">
        <div class="dl">One honest caveat</div>
        <p class="dt">
          “Always disaggregate” is wrong: sometimes the correct answer is in neither table. Simpson
          made the point himself in 1951. He wrote out a table of playing cards, called the combined
          answer the sensible one, then relabelled the identical numbers as a medical trial and
          called the split answer sensible instead. Same arithmetic, opposite verdict. The numbers
          never decide; the story attached to them does. Pearl’s account of exactly when to trust
          which table is argued rather than closed, which is itself worth knowing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Slot>

  {/* The exhibit's last affordance: unfreeze and re-weight from scratch. */}
  <Fragment slot="foot">
    <button class="btn ghost" id="r-reset">↻ Reset the weighting</button>
  </Fragment>
</Shell>

<script>
  import '../scripts/the-reversal';
</script>

<style>
  /* ── Setup table: colour the season each player won (both are Justice) ── */
  td.r-win { color: var(--accent-ink); font-weight: 500 }

  /* ── the playing-time slider ── */
  .rslider-wrap { max-width: 900px; margin: 8px 0 0 }
  .rslider-ends {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 12px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11.5px;
    color: var(--faint);
    letter-spacing: .02em;
    margin-bottom: 9px;
  }
  .rslider-ends b { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 400 }
  .rslider { position: relative; padding: 6px 0 20px }
  .rslider input[type='range'] {
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0;
    accent-color: var(--accent);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .rslider input[type='range']:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: .6 }
  /* the "the total flips here" tick, positioned by the controller */
  .rflip {
    position: absolute;
    top: 2px;
    bottom: 0;
    width: 0;
    border-left: 1px dashed var(--thresh);
    pointer-events: none;
    transform: translateX(-0.5px);
  }
  .rflip-lab {
    position: absolute;
    top: 20px;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10.5px;
    color: var(--faint);
    letter-spacing: .02em;
  }

  /* ── figure legend swatches ── */
  .rsw { width: 11px; height: 11px; border-radius: 50%; flex-shrink: 0; opacity: .6 }
  .rsw-jus { background: #E1583F }
  .rsw-jet { background: #2E9E96 }
  .rdiamond {
    width: 11px;
    height: 11px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    background: var(--muted);
    transform: rotate(45deg);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--panel);
  }
  .lgk { color: var(--faint); white-space: nowrap }

  /* ── Commit: the two name buttons; the picked one goes indigo ── */
  .rnames { gap: 12px }
  .rname.picked {
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--surface);
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }
  .rname.picked:hover { background: var(--accent-ink); color: var(--surface) }
  .rname:disabled { opacity: .4 }
  .rname.picked:disabled { opacity: 1 }

  /* Reveal counter that names a player gets that player's ink, set inline. */
  .counters .cv#r-c-total { letter-spacing: -0.01em }
</style>
